  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🗾 Japanese Sword Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Sword names from real Japanese words, each with its meaning: Tsuki-kage, Kumo-giri, Karasu-maru."
    },
    "intro": "Famous Japanese swords have names that tell a story: <em>Dōjigiri</em>, the \"Child-Cutter\", is said to have killed a <a href=\"demon-name-generator.html\">demon</a>; <em>Mikazuki</em>, \"Crescent Moon\", is named for the crescent shapes in its steel. This **Japanese sword name generator** builds names the same three ways, from real Japanese words, and tells you what each one means.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How famous Japanese swords got their names",
        "content": "<p>Celebrated swords, called <em>meibutsu</em>, usually got their names from a story, a feature of the blade, or an owner, and the smith's name was added after. Five are counted as the greatest, the <strong>Tenka-Goken</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Dōjigiri Yasutsuna</strong>, \"Child-Cutter\", said to have killed the demon Shuten-dōji.</li><li><strong>Mikazuki Munechika</strong>, \"Crescent Moon\", for the crescent-shaped patterns along its edge.</li><li><strong>Ōdenta Mitsuyo</strong>, <strong>Juzumaru Tsunetsugu</strong> (named for a Buddhist rosary, <em>juzu</em>, wrapped round its hilt) and <strong>Onimaru Kunitsuna</strong>, \"Demon\", said to have cut down a demon on its own.</li></ul><p>Others are named for what they cut: <em>Higekiri</em>, \"Beard-Cutter\", and <em>Hizamaru</em>, \"Knee\", tested, the legends say, on condemned prisoners. That's where the \"cutter\" names come from.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Three kinds of name",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Poetic</strong>: two words from nature, like the imagery of a haiku. <em>Tsuki</em> (moon) and <em>kage</em> (shadow) make <em>Tsuki-kage</em>, \"Moon Shadow\".</li><li><strong>Named for what it cut</strong>: a thing, plus <em>-giri</em>, from <em>kiru</em>, to cut. <em>Kumo-giri</em> is \"Cloud Cutter\"; <em>Take-giri</em>, \"Bamboo Cutter\", for a blade that's been tested.</li><li><strong>With -maru</strong>: an old ending found in boys' names, ships' names and swords' names, like the famous <em>Kogarasu-maru</em>, \"Little Crow\".</li></ul><p>The names use hyphens so you can see the parts. Written in Japanese they'd be joined, and sounds sometimes change when words combine.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The legendary smiths",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Masamune</strong>, who worked around 1300, is remembered as Japan's greatest swordsmith, and his name is still used to mean a perfect blade.</li><li><strong>Muramasa</strong>'s swords became known as cursed, said to thirst for blood and to bring misfortune on the Tokugawa family. The legend is much bigger than the history.</li><li>In stories, the two are often set against each other: Masamune's calm perfection against Muramasa's hunger.</li></ul><p>For swords in other traditions, try the <a href=\"sword-name-generator.html\">Sword Name Generator</a> or the <a href=\"elven-sword-name-generator.html\">Elven Sword Name Generator</a>.</p><p>For Chinese martial arts names, see the <a href=\"../categories/wuxia-names.html\">wuxia and xianxia name generators</a>.</p><p>For the swordsman or swordswoman who carries it, try the <a href=\"anime-name-generator.html\">Anime Character Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Are the translations real?",
        "answer": "Yes. Every name is made from real Japanese words, and the meaning shown is what those words mean. They're new combinations, not the names of famous swords."
